                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "48bf58bc-45c8-3f24-ab94-cc57c2b03550",
                                        "entry_id": "3fdb22f1-b01f-3528-af53-f2ccdbc452d7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The late 3rd to early 5th century, the era during which the Sima clan were the legitimate emperors of China."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "45064558-c8e8-35df-a0a3-a6190837a5b7",
                                        "entry_id": "3fdb22f1-b01f-3528-af53-f2ccdbc452d7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A river (Jin River, 晉江／晋江 (Jìnjiāng)) in Quanzhou Municipality, Fujian, China, emptying into the Taiwan Strait."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "226940c5-3495-40df-9bcb-27b8caedbaea",
                                                "sense_id": "45064558-c8e8-35df-a0a3-a6190837a5b7",
                                                "text": "Between 1946 and 1948 in the ruins of the foundations of Quanzhou’s South Gate Tower and southern wall we excavated more that twenty large white gravestones, inscribed in Arabic on both sides. They obviously had been taken from a demolished Islamic mosque. The area overlooked in the Jin River and was known as South Quanzhou, or the “Foreign District.” It was here that merchants from overseas and their families had lived during Song and Yuan."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21bd9748-4004-4ab6-95ad-9bb9c674c2c2",
                                                "sense_id": "45064558-c8e8-35df-a0a3-a6190837a5b7",
                                                "text": "Wang Feng and I made our way to the world’s former greatest harbour. It is to the west of the city on the Jin River, and far enough inland from Quanzhou Bay to be reasonably safe from typhoons."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ba861abc-d5d6-4a51-a25a-c5bdb5b45c5c",
                                                "sense_id": "45064558-c8e8-35df-a0a3-a6190837a5b7",
                                                "text": "Located on the Jin River, Quanzhou was China’s principal port during the Song and Yuan dynasties."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0b1aa623-80c5-4521-946b-f0a70682ce42",
                                                "sense_id": "45064558-c8e8-35df-a0a3-a6190837a5b7",
                                                "text": "He said that four years ago, the island began receiving water from the Jin River, in Quanzhou, China, and now 70-80% of Kinmen's water comes from China."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
